# The terminal a Gridline session opens.
#
# Without this the choice is arbitrary. The resolver walks every application on
# the machine in the order the directory scan produced and takes the first
# carrying the TerminalEmulator category. Measured on the baseline: 114
# candidates, with three terminals at positions 9, 76 and 102. Installing or
# removing any unrelated application can change which one a Gridline keybinding
# opens.
#
# The filename is keyed on Gridline's desktop name, which is how the resolver
# finds it, and it is why this preference applies in a Gridline session and in
# no other. A machine's own terminal choice is left alone, which is the
# difference between expressing a preference and taking one.
#
# Two entries, and the second is not decoration.
#
# This file held one, and said so for a reason that has stopped being true:
# "the terminal named here is a dependency of gridline-session, so a machine
# without it has no Gridline session to express a preference for, and a second
# line would be unreachable." The terminal is a recommendation now, so a
# Gridline session can perfectly well run on a machine where the first entry
# resolves to nothing, and the second line is exactly the case that used to be
# unreachable.
#
# What happens without it was measured rather than assumed, and the first
# reading was too kind. With the preferred terminal removed the session still
# opened one, which looked like a safe fallback; it was the directory scan, and
# the scan was right only because that machine had two terminals installed and
# one had just been taken away. Nothing names a terminal for a Gridline
# session: the desktop-keyed lists the resolver ships are keyed on other
# desktops, and the first element of this session's desktop identity is
# Gridline, which none of them matches.
#
# Installing xterm settled it. That package has nothing to do with this
# project; it was installed only so the scan would have a third candidate and
# could disagree with this file. It brought two entries, and the scan then
# picked one that needs X11 and fails in a Wayland session. The key opened
# nothing at all - no window and no message - and the cause was an unrelated
# apt install. With the second entry below present, the same removal opened the
# terminal named here.
#
# So the second entry is the project's answer rather than the scan's. It names
# what Ubuntu lists for itself, which is the right second choice on the desktop
# Gridline is built for, and it carries the same :new-window argument Ubuntu
# uses so that opening a terminal does not reuse an existing window.
kitty.desktop
org.gnome.Ptyxis.desktop:new-window
